  <h2>When a ductless mini-split is the right tool</h2>
  <p>A <strong>mini-split installation</strong> is not a replacement for a well-designed central system in most homes; for whole-home comfort, our <a href="/services/hvac-installation-tampa/">HVAC installation</a> and <a href="/services/ac-replacement-tampa/">AC replacement</a> pages cover ducted systems. But for the specific problems ductless solves, nothing else comes close. These are the five scenarios where we recommend it without hesitation:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Garage conversion or workshop:</strong> a 12,000 BTU single-zone head turns a 400 sq ft garage into a usable office, gym, or shop for roughly $4,200 installed.</li>
    <li><strong>Sunroom or bonus room:</strong> a west-facing glass sunroom in <a href="/service-areas/lutz-home-air-conditioning-service/">Lutz</a> or <a href="/service-areas/new-tampa-heating-and-cooling/">New Tampa</a> can run 15 degrees hotter than the house. A dedicated 18,000 BTU head fixes what duct extension cannot.</li>
    <li><strong>Mother-in-law suite or ADU:</strong> the cleanest way to condition a 300 to 800 sq ft accessory unit without tying into the main system.</li>
    <li><strong>Addition or second story:</strong> a dedicated head keeps your original system in its sweet spot instead of overstretching the ductwork.</li>
    <li><strong>Whole-home ductless:</strong> for older homes with no ductwork, a 3 or 4-zone system conditions the whole house with zero duct construction.</li>
  </ul>

  <TrustBar />

  <h2>Tampa Bay sizing rules we actually use</h2>
  <p>Manufacturer charts assume national-average climate. Florida is not average, so we size for a 94-degree design day, 78-degree dew point, and 2,200 annual cooling hours. The head you need is usually one size larger than the square-footage chart suggests:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>9,000 BTU:</strong> 300 to 450 sq ft, a well-insulated bedroom or office with one exterior wall.</li>
    <li><strong>12,000 BTU:</strong> 450 to 650 sq ft, a living area with two exterior walls or an east-glass sunroom.</li>
    <li><strong>18,000 BTU:</strong> 700 to 950 sq ft, an open living-kitchen combo or a west-facing sunroom.</li>
    <li><strong>24,000 BTU:</strong> 950 to 1,300 sq ft, large open spaces, usually one zone in a multi-zone install.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are starting points, not quotes. The real load depends on ceiling height, insulation, windows, orientation, and internal heat gain, so we run a short Manual-J on every quote, the same calculation behind our <a href="/services/ac-installation-wesley-chapel/">Wesley Chapel AC installs</a>.</p>

  <InContentCTA
    heading="Got a room that never gets cool?"
    text="Tell us about the space and we will tell you whether a single head solves it or whether the fix is somewhere else. In-home assessment, no pressure."
    tone="brand"
  />

  <h2>A Florida garage runs hotter than its square footage suggests</h2>
  <p>Garages are the most common ductless request we get after additions, and they are also where sizing goes wrong most often. A Wesley Chapel or Tampa garage carries heat loads that a bedroom of the same footprint never sees:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Uninsulated block walls:</strong> most Florida garages stop at bare concrete block, no batt and no foam, so the wall stores afternoon heat and releases it into the room until well after sunset.</li>
    <li><strong>A west-facing metal door:</strong> an uninsulated steel overhead door in full afternoon sun acts like a radiator for the last four hours of daylight. On most west-facing garages it is the single largest load in the space.</li>
    <li><strong>Attic heat pressing down:</strong> garage ceilings are usually uninsulated too, with a very hot attic sitting directly above the drywall.</li>
    <li><strong>Leakage at the door track:</strong> the gap around an overhead door never seals the way a wall does, so outdoor humidity keeps arriving all day and the head has to work on latent load, not just temperature.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>On an August afternoon we routinely measure garages 15 to 25 degrees above the conditioned rooms on the other side of the wall. Added together, those loads push the capacity you need a full size above the square-footage bands above, so we shift one band up for any attached garage:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Two-car garage, roughly 400 to 480 sq ft:</strong> 12,000 BTU, not the 9,000 BTU the same footprint would take as a bedroom.</li>
    <li><strong>Two-car with a west door or a finished ceiling above a hot attic:</strong> 12,000 BTU minimum, and we check the door orientation before committing.</li>
    <li><strong>Three-car garage, roughly 600 to 750 sq ft:</strong> 18,000 BTU.</li>
  </ul>
  <img src="/images/air-conditioning-not-cooling-tampa-fl-mini-split-outdoor-condenser-unit-wall-mounted.webp" alt="Wall-mounted mini-split outdoor condenser installed on a Tampa home exterior" title="Wall-mounted mini-split condenser on a Tampa install" width="900" height="560" loading="lazy" class="w-full h-auto rounded-2xl ring-1 ring-slate-200 shadow-[var(--shadow-card)]" />
  <p>A home gym holds 72 degrees against body heat and equipment on a 12,000 BTU head once the door is shut. A workshop works too, though saw dust means rinsing the filter more often than a living room would need. A home office is the conversion we see most in <a href="/service-areas/wesley-chapel-ac-repair/">Wesley Chapel</a> and <a href="/service-areas/new-tampa-heating-and-cooling/">New Tampa</a>. One limit is worth hearing before you buy: no mini-split cools a garage with the overhead door open. If you plan to work with the door up in July, a good fan does more for you than a compressor will.</p>
  <p>Cost lands where the single-zone numbers below land: about $4,200 for a 12,000 BTU head in a standard two-car garage, more if the panel needs a new circuit run out to the space. Adding insulation to the door and the ceiling first will let you use a smaller head and lower the running cost, and we will say so during the assessment rather than sell you the bigger unit.</p>

  <h2>Brands we install, ranked by Tampa field reliability</h2>
  <p>These rankings come from the several hundred mini-splits on our maintenance rolls across <a href="/service-areas/pasco-county-ac-repair/">Pasco</a>, <a href="/service-areas/hillsborough-county-hvac-company/">Hillsborough</a>, and Polk counties:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mitsubishi Electric:</strong> best overall reliability in Florida humidity, strongest parts pipeline, cleanest installs. Our default for most homes.</li>
    <li><strong>Fujitsu Halcyon:</strong> very close to Mitsubishi on reliability, often better pricing, excellent multi-zone flexibility.</li>
    <li><strong>LG:</strong> strong mid-tier, especially the ArtCool designer heads where looks matter.</li>
    <li><strong>Daikin:</strong> solid value, most reliable in the 18k and 24k sizes.</li>
    <li><strong>Gree:</strong> the budget end we will install, only when a homeowner understands the slower warranty support.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Whichever brand fits, the <a href="/services/ac-repair-tampa/">repair</a> and <a href="/services/ac-maintenance-tampa/">maintenance</a> support behind it matters more than the badge, and we service what we install.</p>

  <ReviewQuote
